
## Output Format

- A SymboleoAC contract has this exact top-level structure. Everything lives inside `endContract`; nothing valid follows it:

  Domain <domainName>
    <type definitions>
  endDomain

  Contract <ContractName> (<param>: <Type>, ...)
  Declarations
    <var>: <DomainType> with <attr> := <value>, ...;
  Obligations
    <name>: <O | Obligation>(debtor, creditor, antecedent, consequent);
  Powers
    <name>: <P | Power>(creditor, debtor, antecedent, consequent);
  endContract

{# The placement rules below are JAR-verified and pinned by
   tests/integration/test_placement_rules.py, with a phrase in _PLACEMENT_RULES
   (tests/unit/test_prompts.py) so deleting one reds. That is binding on any
   rule added from here on; a few older structural bullets predate it and carry
   neither. See CLAUDE.md, "Leaf-construct policy", for the cap. #}
- Sections may only appear in this order: `Declarations`, `Preconditions`, `Postconditions`, `Obligations`, `Surviving Obligations`, `Powers`, `ACPolicy`, `Constraints`. All are optional except `Obligations`, whose header is required even for a contract that has only powers — write the header with nothing under it. The parameter list must hold at least two parameters.
- Define every party, asset, and event in the Domain before use. Roles, assets, and events are domain types: `<Name> isA Role with <attr>: <Type>, ...;`, `<Name> isAn Asset with ...;`, `<Name> isAn Event with ...;`. The article is fixed for the other two forms: a fixed value set is always `<Name> isAn Enumeration(A, B, C);`, and a base-type alias is always `<Name> isA Number;` (likewise `String`, `Date`, `Boolean`).
- Name an enumeration value by qualifying it with its type — `delivered.quality == MeatQuality(PRIME)`, never a bare `PRIME` — in comparisons and `:=` bindings alike.
- In Declarations, each variable's type must be a domain type defined above (e.g. a Role) — never a grammar rule name or a base type. Bind contract parameters to attributes with `:=`. Every declared variable is an instance of a domain type, so there are no standalone values: `<name>: Date := ...` and `<name> := ...` are both invalid.
- A date is therefore an attribute of a domain type, computed where it is bound: declare `Delivered isAn Event with delDueDate: Date, ...;`, bind `delivered: Delivered with delDueDate := Date.add(effDate, delDueDateDays, days), ...;`, and refer to it later as `delivered.delDueDate`. `Date.add(<date>, <amount>, <timeUnit>)` is the only date-arithmetic construct; `<timeUnit>` is a bare keyword (`seconds`, `minutes`, `hours`, `days`, `weeks`, `months`, `years`). Do not invent infix forms like `date + N days`.
- `Date.add(...)` may appear only where a value is bound (`<attr> := Date.add(...)`) or where a predicate expects a time point (`WhappensBefore`, `ShappensBefore`, `HappensAfter`, `Interval`). It may NOT appear in a comparison: write `WhappensBefore(delivered, Date.add(effDate, delDueDateDays, days))`, not `delivered.delDueDate == Date.add(effDate, delDueDateDays, days)`.
- A literal date is `Date("yyyy/MM/dd HH:mm:ss")` — slashes, and the time is required: `Date("2026/01/31 00:00:00")`. `Date(...)` builds a literal from a quoted string and never wraps a value you already have: pass an existing Date parameter or attribute directly, never `Date(effDate)`.
- Obligations express duties ("shall", "must"): `O(debtor, creditor, antecedent, consequent)` — choose `O` OR `Obligation`, never both. The debtor owes the duty to the creditor.
- Powers express rights that change contract state ("may terminate / suspend / resume"): `P(creditor, debtor, antecedent, consequent)`. The role order is REVERSED from obligations — the party holding the right is the creditor and comes first. The consequent is a state change such as `Terminated(self)`, `Suspended(obligations.<name>)`, `Resumed(obligations.<name>)`, or `Triggered(obligations.<name>)`. `obligations.` there is a required literal prefix, not a path you may shorten — write `Suspended(obligations.deliverGoods)`, never `Suspended(deliverGoods)`; a norm named in any predicate is likewise `obligations.<name>` or `powers.<name>`.
- Those state changes belong to powers alone: an obligation's consequent may never be `Suspended(...)`, `Resumed(...)`, `Terminated(...)` or `Triggered(...)`, so model even a mandatory "shall suspend" as a power. An obligation that updates a value wraps the assignment: `Assign(<var>.<attr> := <value>)`, or `HappensAssign(<event>, <var>.<attr> := <value>)` to tie it to an event — never a bare `<var>.<attr> := <value>`.
- Antecedents, consequents, and triggers are inline propositions built from predicates over declared events — e.g. `Happens(<event>)`, `WhappensBefore(<event>, <point>)`, `Happens(Violated(obligations.<name>))`. Never reference a separately named proposition, and never use quoted English prose as a proposition.
- An optional `<trigger> -> ` prefix gates when an obligation or power activates: `<name>: <trigger> -> O(...)`.
